{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "11-fixed-points-in-practice.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Fixed points in practice",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Recognise \"apply until nothing changes\" as computing a least fixed point, compute one on a lattice of sets, and see why it stops and why it finds exactly what is forced.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Which pages can a crawler reach from the home page? Which facts follow from a set of rules? Which variables might be unset at some line of a program? The algorithm is the same loop each time: start with what you know, apply the step, add what it gives, and stop when it gives nothing new. Lesson 4 said when such a loop stops and what it finds. Here it is at work.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Reachability",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The nodes reachable from $a$ in the graph $a \\to b \\to c$, $d \\to a$ are the least set $S$ with $a \\in S$ and closed under successors: the least fixed point of $f(S) = \\{a\\} \\cup S \\cup \\mathrm{succ}(S)$ on the subsets of $\\{a, b, c, d\\}$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let P4 = subsets({a, b, c, d})",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let reach = map(P4; {}->{a}, {a}->{a,b}, {b}->{a,b,c}, {c}->{a,c}, {d}->{a,d}, {a,b}->{a,b,c}, {a,c}->{a,b,c}, {a,d}->{a,b,d}, {b,c}->{a,b,c}, {b,d}->{a,b,c,d}, {c,d}->{a,c,d}, {a,b,d}->{a,b,c,d}, {a,c,d}->{a,b,c,d}, {b,c,d}->{a,b,c,d})",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "monotone(P4, reach)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Step through the iteration. Before each step, say which nodes the next set adds.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "lfp(P4, reach)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "Each step adds one more layer of successors: $\\varnothing$, $\\{a\\}$, $\\{a, b\\}$, $\\{a, b, c\\}$, and then nothing changes. $d$ is not reachable from $a$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Why start at the bottom",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The reachable set is a fixed point: the step adds nothing to it. It is not the only one.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fixpoints(P4, reach)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "gfp(P4, reach)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$\\{a, b, c, d\\}$ is closed under successors too: no edge leads out of it. Iterating down from the top stops there at once, and the answer contains $d$, which is not reachable. The least fixed point contains only what is forced, and starting from $\\bot$ is what finds it: every set in the chain from $\\bot$ is below every fixed point, so the chain cannot overshoot.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Why it stops",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!theorem] The ascending chain condition\n> If every increasing chain $x_0 \\le x_1 \\le \\cdots$ in a lattice with a bottom is eventually constant (always true on a finite lattice), then iterating a monotone $f$ from $\\bot$ reaches a fixed point after finitely many steps, and it is the least one.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "On the subsets of an $n$-element set a strictly increasing chain has at most $n + 1$ sets, so the iteration takes at most $n$ steps that change anything. In Lean, every iterate is below every fixed point above the start:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem iter_le_of_fixed {α : Type} [PartialOrder α] {f : α → α} (hf : Monotone f) {a x : α}\n    (ha : a ≤ x) (hx : f x = x) : ∀ n, iter f n a ≤ x := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove that every iterate of $f$ from $a$ stays below a fixed point $x$ above $a$.",
      "leanSolution": "  intro n\n  induction n with\n  | zero => exact ha\n  | succ n ih =>\n    show f (iter f n a) ≤ x\n    rw [← hx]\n    exact hf.map_le _ _ ih",
      "hints": [
        "Induction on `n`. The base case is `ha`.",
        "For `n + 1`, the goal is `f (iter f n a) ≤ x`; rewrite `x` as `f x` and use monotonicity on the hypothesis."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let R3 = map(P4; {}->{d}, {a}->{a,b,d}, {b}->{b,c,d}, {c}->{c,d}, {d}->{a,d}, {a,b}->{a,b,c,d}, {a,c}->{a,b,c,d}, {a,d}->{a,b,d}, {b,c}->{b,c,d}, {b,d}->{a,b,c,d}, {c,d}->{a,c,d}, {a,b,c}->{a,b,c,d}, {a,b,d}->{a,b,c,d}, {a,c,d}->{a,b,c,d}, {b,c,d}->{a,b,c,d})",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "lfp(P4, R3)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "`R3` is the reachability step from $d$. What can $d$ reach? Write the set, such as `{a, b}`.",
      "hints": [
        "Iterate from the empty set: $\\{d\\}$ first."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fixpoints(P4, R3)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "List every fixed point of `R3`, as a set of sets. Why is there only one this time?",
      "hints": [
        "A fixed point contains $d$ and is closed under successors.",
        "From $d$ every node is reachable, so the only such set is all of them: `{{a,b,c,d}}`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> \"Apply until nothing changes\" computes a least fixed point when the step is monotone and starts from the bottom. On a finite lattice it always stops, and what it finds is the smallest solution: everything forced, nothing more. Starting from the top finds the greatest, which can contain things nothing forced.\n\nThat was one set and one step. Next: many cells, each holding part of an answer, and many small steps reading and writing them, in whatever order a scheduler picks.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
